---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Topbar from '../components/Topbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import SubscribeForm from '../components/SubscribeForm.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

const description =
  'The cross-org aggregate of verification cost. Not published yet — here is what it will ' +
  'contain and what has to be true before it ships.';
---

<Base title="The aggregate report — verification-tax" description={description} path="/report">
  <Topbar />

  <main id="main" class="page">
    <h1>The aggregate report</h1>
    <p class="stamp">NOT PUBLISHED YET</p>

    <p>
      There is no report on this page because there is not yet enough data to write one that would
      survive being read carefully. This page exists so that the claim on the home page has
      somewhere honest to point.
    </p>

    <h2>What it will be</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The distribution of review load, rework rate, time to first review and unreviewed merge rate
        across every org that opted in.
      </li>
      <li>
        The full dataset attached, as the twelve integers per org and nothing more, so anyone can
        redo the analysis and disagree with it.
      </li>
      <li>The method, the coefficients, and every place the model is doing something arguable.</li>
      <li>Free, on this page, with no email required to read it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What has to be true first</h2>
    <p>
      Enough opted-in orgs that the percentiles are not just describing a handful of companies who
      already knew they had a problem. Until then the tool reports the sample size next to every
      percentile, so you can discount it yourself.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <b>The sample is self-selected and that will not stop being true.</b> Orgs that go looking for
        a review problem are not a random draw from the industry. The report will say so at the top, not
        in a footnote, and the raw data will be there for anyone who wants to argue about it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Tell me when it lands</h2>
    <p>One email, when the report is out. No sequence, no drip, no newsletter.</p>
    <SubscribeForm />
    <p class="mail-note">
      Unsubscribe is a link, not a conversation. What happens to the address is on <a
        href="/privacy">the privacy page</a
      >.
    </p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
